<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 10th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> · Also known as King David</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">David was, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, the second king of the united kingdom of Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a> and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, who made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> his capital. He is remembered as warrior, psalmist and founder of the royal line of Judah. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele">Tel Dan stele</a> of the 9th century BCE refers to a '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david.jpg" alt="David" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerard van Honthorst · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_David,_the_King_of_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
